Below are ten exploratory Snowflake SQL snippets (each limited to 20 rows) that go from very simple inspection to progressively more complex operations that would ultimately culminate in the final computation.  
Every column name is placed in double quotes, and comments explain the purpose of each query.

```sql
--Description: 1) Peek at a handful of Nature articles to understand basic fields.
SELECT "id",
       "date",
       "title",
       LEFT("body", 120) AS "body_preview"
FROM WORD_VECTORS_US.WORD_VECTORS_US.NATURE
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 2) Retrieve the single article we must compare others against.
SELECT  "id",
        "date",
        "title",
        LEFT("body", 500) AS "body_excerpt"
FROM WORD_VECTORS_US.WORD_VECTORS_US.NATURE
WHERE "id" = '8a78ef2d-d5f7-4d2d-9b47-5adb25cbd373'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 3) See distinct publication dates available in the dataset.
SELECT DISTINCT "date"
FROM WORD_VECTORS_US.WORD_VECTORS_US.NATURE
ORDER BY "date" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 4) List some distinct authors strings to gauge format / delimiters.
SELECT DISTINCT "authors"
FROM WORD_VECTORS_US.WORD_VECTORS_US.NATURE
WHERE "authors" IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 5) Quick inspection of the WORD_FREQUENCIES table (word & counts).
SELECT "word",
       "frequency"
FROM WORD_VECTORS_US.WORD_VECTORS_US.WORD_FREQUENCIES
ORDER BY "frequency" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 6) Sample a few word vectors (300‑D stored in VARIANT) to see shape.
SELECT "word",
       "vector"
FROM WORD_VECTORS_US.WORD_VECTORS_US.GLOVE_VECTORS
ORDER BY RANDOM()
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 7) Tokenise the body of the target article (stop‑words removed).
SELECT  token AS "word"
FROM WORD_VECTORS_US.WORD_VECTORS_US.NATURE n,
     LATERAL FLATTEN(
         input =>  spider2-public-data.word_vectors_us.tokenise_no_stop(n."body")
     ) f(token)
WHERE n."id" = '8a78ef2d-d5f7-4d2d-9b47-5adb25cbd373'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 8) Join those tokens to WORD_FREQUENCIES to get frequency counts.
SELECT  tok.token::STRING                AS "word",
        wf."frequency"
FROM WORD_VECTORS_US.WORD_VECTORS_US.NATURE n,
     LATERAL FLATTEN(
         input =>  spider2-public-data.word_vectors_us.tokenise_no_stop(n."body")
     ) tok
JOIN WORD_VECTORS_US.WORD_VECTORS_US.WORD_FREQUENCIES wf
  ON wf."word" = tok.token::STRING
WHERE n."id" = '8a78ef2d-d5f7-4d2d-9b47-5adb25cbd373'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 9) Extend previous join to pull the GloVe vector for each token.
SELECT  tok.token::STRING                AS "word",
        wf."frequency",
        gv."vector"
FROM WORD_VECTORS_US.WORD_VECTORS_US.NATURE n,
     LATERAL FLATTEN(
         input =>  spider2-public-data.word_vectors_us.tokenise_no_stop(n."body")
     ) tok
JOIN WORD_VECTORS_US.WORD_VECTORS_US.WORD_FREQUENCIES wf
  ON wf."word" = tok.token::STRING
JOIN WORD_VECTORS_US.WORD_VECTORS_US.GLOVE_VECTORS gv
  ON gv."word" = tok.token::STRING
WHERE n."id" = '8a78ef2d-d5f7-4d2d-9b47-5adb25cbd373'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 10) Prototype the weighting formula (vector ÷ frequency^0.4) 
--               for one article & show the first weighted vector element.
SELECT  gv."word",
        wf."frequency",
        gv."vector"[0]               AS first_dim_original,
        gv."vector"[0] / POW(wf."frequency", 0.4) AS first_dim_weighted
FROM WORD_VECTORS_US.WORD_VECTORS_US.NATURE n,
     LATERAL FLATTEN(
         input =>  spider2-public-data.word_vectors_us.tokenise_no_stop(n."body")
     ) tok
JOIN WORD_VECTORS_US.WORD_VECTORS_US.WORD_FREQUENCIES wf
  ON wf."word" = tok.token::STRING
JOIN WORD_VECTORS_US.WORD_VECTORS_US.GLOVE_VECTORS gv
  ON gv."word" = tok.token::STRING
WHERE n."id" = '8a78ef2d-d5f7-4d2d-9b47-5adb25cbd373'
LIMIT 20;
```

These queries progress from simple inspection to the essential joins and weighting step that precede computing aggregate vectors, normalisation, cosine similarities, and ultimately listing the top‑10 similar articles.
